+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by impact. Items 1 to 5 are bugs or exploits that will show up in
+play. Items 6 to 8 are design problems that get more expensive the more you build on them.
+Items 9 and 10 are style and typing cleanups.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Remote handlers trust what the client sends
+
+**Where:** lines 84, 105, 127 (the three `OnServerEvent` handlers).
+
+**What's wrong:** The handler parameters are annotated `bt: string`, `amt: number`,
+`pos: Vector3`, `part: Instance`, but annotations do nothing at runtime. An exploiter can
+send any value. Concrete holes:
+
+- **NaN coins (line 85 to 94).** Every comparison with NaN is false, so `amt = 0/0` passes
+  `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE`, `price` becomes NaN, `coins < price` is false, and
+  `setCoins` writes NaN to the player's DataStore entry. Their stock for that type also
+  becomes NaN.
+- **Fractional purchases (line 85).** `amt = 1.5` is accepted, which gives fractional
+  stock and fractional coin balances.
+- **Unknown block type (line 89).** A `bt` that isn't in `PRICES`, or isn't a string,
+  makes `PRICES[bt] * amt` throw. It does no damage, but any client can make the handler
+  error whenever it wants.
+- **NaN position skips the reach and height checks (line 111 to 112).** A `pos` with a NaN
+  component snaps to a NaN center. In `PlacementRules.check`, `center.Y < 0`,
+  `center.Y > MaxHeight`, and `Magnitude > MaxReach` are all false for NaN, so the
+  placement is accepted from anywhere. A non-Vector3 `pos` makes `snap` throw.
+- **`part` can be anything (line 128).** `nil` or a non-Instance throws on `part.Parent`.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type every argument after `player` as `unknown` and narrow it
+before use, returning early on failure. Shape range checks so they also reject NaN.
+
+```lua
+local function onBuyBlocks(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- rejects NaN, inf, and fractions
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	-- blockType is now safe to cast to BlockTypes.BlockType
+	...
+end
+```
+
+For placement, check `typeof(position) == "Vector3"` and that each component is finite
+(`x == x and math.abs(x) ~= math.huge`) before snapping. The finite check should also go
+into `PlacementRules.check`, so the shared rule rejects NaN for every caller. For removal,
+check `typeof(part) == "Instance"` and `part:IsA("BasePart")`.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Coins: a DataStore round trip on every purchase, with a race and no error handling
+
+**Where:** lines 42 to 48 (`getCoins` / `setCoins`), used at lines 90 to 94.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Double spend.** `GetAsync` yields. If a client fires `BuyBlocks` twice quickly, both
+  handlers read the same balance before either writes it, both pass the `coins < price`
+  check, and both grant stock. The player pays once and gets two orders.
+- **Coins lost if the player leaves mid-purchase.** If the player leaves during
+  `GetAsync`, `PlayerRemoving` clears `stock[plr]`. Then `setCoins` deducts the coins and
+  line 97 throws indexing `nil`, so the player paid and got nothing.
+- **No `pcall`.** `GetAsync` and `SetAsync` throw on throttling, outages, and in Studio
+  without API access. Today that just kills the handler, but nothing reports it and
+  nothing retries.
+- **Request budget.** Every buy spends two DataStore requests, so a client spamming the
+  remote can use up the server's DataStore budget for everyone.
+
+**What to do instead:** Load each player's coins once when they join (wrapped in `pcall`,
+with retries), keep the balance in a server-side session table, and change it in memory.
+Purchases then don't yield, so the check-and-deduct can't race. Save with `UpdateAsync` on
+`PlayerRemoving`, on a periodic autosave, and in `game:BindToClose`. Rename the yielding
+functions `loadCoinsAsync` / `saveCoinsAsync`. If you plan to store more than coins, use a
+session-locking profile library rather than writing this yourself.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Removing an abandoned block throws, and the ownership check compares names
+
+**Where:** lines 50 to 57 (`getOwnerName`) and lines 133 to 136.
+
+**What's wrong:** `getOwnerName` falls off its end when no player matches, so it returns
+*no values*, not `nil`. `tostring()` with no argument throws "missing argument #1". So the
+case the comment describes ("anyone can clear away a block whose owner has left") always
+errors. Only owners can ever remove blocks. The check also turns the result into the
+string `"nil"` and compares display names, when the part already stores a `UserId`.
+
+**What to do instead:** Compare user IDs directly and drop `getOwnerName`:
+
+```lua
+const ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+const isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+const isOwnerGone = typeof(ownerId) ~= "number" or Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) == nil
+if not isOwner and not isOwnerGone then
+	return
+end
+```
+
+---
+
+## 4. Clearing someone else's block refunds it to you
+
+**Where:** line 139.
+
+**What's wrong:** Once item 3 is fixed, the refund goes to whoever removed the block. A
+player can walk around clearing blocks that departed players paid for and collect them as
+free stock. It also uses `part.Name` as the block type, so anything that renames a placed
+part breaks the refund.
+
+**What to do instead:** Refund only when `isOwner` is true. Clearing an abandoned block
+should just destroy it. Store the type as an attribute (`part:SetAttribute("BlockType",
+blockType)`) and read that back, checking it with `BlockTypes.isBlockType`.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Players can never turn receipts off
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS` replaces
+`false` with `true`, so a player who sets `ShowReceipts = false` still gets receipts. The
+`AutoAnchor` code at lines 118 to 121 handles this correctly. Neither attribute is
+type-checked: a non-boolean `AutoAnchor` makes `part.Anchored = value` throw.
+
+**What to do instead:** Use one helper for both:
+
+```lua
+local function getBooleanAttribute(instance: Instance, name: string, default: boolean): boolean
+	const value = instance:GetAttribute(name)
+	return if typeof(value) == "boolean" then value else default
+end
+```
+
+---
+
+## 6. Players who joined before the script ran have no stock table
+
+**Where:** lines 142 to 144, and every `stock[plr]` index (lines 96, 106, 139).
+
+**What's wrong:** `PlayerAdded` is connected at the bottom of the script, after the
+module-level setup. Players who are already in the server when it connects, which is
+common in Studio play-solo, never get `stock[plr] = {}`, and every remote they fire
+throws. The map is typed `{ [Player]: { [string]: number } }`, so the type checker
+can't see that a lookup might be `nil`.
+
+**What to do instead:** Run the join handler for existing players as well as new ones,
+and type the map so missing keys are checked:
+
+```lua
+local stockByPlayer: { [Player]: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }? } = {}
+
+Players.PlayerAdded:Connect(onPlayerAdded)
+for _, player in Players:GetPlayers() do
+	task.spawn(onPlayerAdded, player)
+end
+```
+
+Each handler then does `const playerStock = stockByPlayer[player]` and returns early if
+it's `nil`.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Bought stock is never saved
+
+**Where:** lines 40 and 146 to 148.
+
+**What's wrong:** Coins are saved (item 2), but the blocks they buy only exist in memory
+and are deleted on `PlayerRemoving`. A player who buys 50 bricks and leaves before placing
+them loses the coins and the bricks. Placed blocks also stay in the world after their
+owner leaves, and item 4 lets other players turn them into stock.
+
+**What to do instead:** Decide what a purchase should give the player across sessions,
+then build it into the same session data as coins (item 2). Either save stock alongside
+coins, or refund unplaced stock on leave. Also decide what happens to placed blocks when
+the owner leaves (keep, destroy, or refund to the owner), and make removal (items 3 and 4)
+match.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Client-triggered log spam
+
+**Where:** line 114 (`warn` on every failed placement) and line 79 (`print` on every
+placement).
+
+**What's wrong:** Both run once per remote call, so any client can flood the server
+output and the developer console. Failed placements are normal play (out of reach,
+occupied), not warnings.
+
+**What to do instead:** Remove both, or log only behind a debug flag. If the player needs
+to know why a placement failed, send `reason` back to them, ideally rate-limited.
+Consider a simple per-player rate limit on all three remotes.
+
+---
+
+## 9. `placeBlock` signature
+
+**Where:** lines 59 to 65 and the call at line 124.
+
+**What's wrong:** Five positional parameters, two of them booleans
+(`placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)`). You can't tell from the call site what
+`true` means. `log` is always `true`, and `bt` is typed `string` and then cast at line 66,
+which hides any caller that passes an unchecked type.
+
+**What to do instead:** Remove `log` (see item 8). Take `blockType: BlockTypes.BlockType`
+so callers have to validate first, and pass `Anchored` in an options table:
+`placeBlock(player, center, blockType, { Anchored = isAutoAnchor })`.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Style and typing cleanups
+
+These are all from the project's Luau style guide. StyLua (`stylua.toml`) covers layout
+but not these.
+
+- **Line 1:** add `--!strict` as the first line. The shared modules already have it.
+- **Lines 3 to 22:** bind services, requires, and constants with `const`.
+- **Names:** spell them out. `plr` → `player`, `amt` → `amount`, `bt` → `blockType`,
+  `pos` → `position`, `p` → `otherPlayer`, `stock` → `stockByPlayer`. Name boolean locals
+  as questions (`shouldShowReceipts`, `isAutoAnchor`).
+- **Lines 14 to 20:** type `PRICES` by block type, not `string`, so a block type without a
+  price is a type error:
+  `const PRICES: { [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number } = { ... }`. Or move `Price` into
+  `BlockStyle` in `BlockTypes` so each type's data lives in one place.
+- **Lines 84, 105, 127, 142, 146:** give the handlers names (`onBuyBlocks`,
+  `onPlaceBlock`, `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`) and connect them
+  at the bottom. That keeps the connections in one place and makes it easy to run
+  `onPlayerAdded` for existing players (item 6).
+- **Lines 42, 46:** functions that yield end in `Async` (covered by item 2).
+- **Lines 24 to 34:** the remotes are created at runtime, so client code has to
+  `WaitForChild` every one of them. If you'd rather not, declare them in
+  `default.project.json` instead. This is a matter of preference, not a bug.
+
+---
+
+## Suggested order of work
+
+Items 1 to 6 should be fixed together, since they all touch the three handlers and the
+per-player state. Decide item 7 before writing the session-data code for item 2, because
+the answer changes what gets saved. Items 8 to 10 fit into the same rewrite at little extra
+cost.
